Name: Tyler McFarland
Date: 10/14/26
Environment: opsys (linux), vi, g++, git

How to compile the project:
Type 'make'

Example of how to run the project:
./oss -n 3 -s 2 -t 4 -i 0.6 -f log.txt

Options:
-n  total number of workers to launch
-s  most workers running at the same time (max 20)
-t  workers get a random run time between 1 second and this
-i  minimum simulated time between launching workers
-f  log file for oss output

How it works:
oss keeps a simulated clock in shared memory and a process table of 20 PCBs.
oss sends a message to one worker at a time (round robin through the process table)
using a message queue, with mtype set to the worker's pid. The worker checks the clock,
and sends back 1 if it is still running or 0 if it is terminating (mtype set to oss's pid).
The clock goes up by 250ms divided by the number of running workers each loop.
oss stops after 60 real seconds or ctrl-c, kills the workers, and frees shared memory and the message queue.

